1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,320
It was a totally radical decade that realized the transition from the industrial age to the

2
00:00:08,321 --> 00:00:15,859
information age. An era of awesome acronyms. CD, MTV, VCR, IBM, PC.

3
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:18,560
A technological paradise

4
00:00:18,561 --> 00:00:23,527
dedicated to entertainment and ergonomics, courtesy of Silicon Valley.

5
00:00:23,528 --> 00:00:24,520
Hold on tight,

6
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:26,680
because we're going back to that future.

7
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:31,900
Now, 80s Tech on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:51,160
It was a decade that rocked our world in many ways.

9
00:00:54,520 --> 00:01:01,060
A shuttle exploded, shocking America and grounding its space program.

10
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:05,920
A pandemic was identified, stirring social controversy.

11
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:11,460
Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall.

12
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:16,920
A wall came down, ending the 40-year Cold War.

13
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:20,980
And for the first time, the man of the year was a machine.

14
00:01:20,981 --> 00:01:26,480
It was the 1980s, and despite some tumultuous events,

15
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:29,940
it's remembered as a time of optimism and prosperity.

16
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:34,120
Money seemed to be pouring out of the sky for a lot of people,

17
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:38,300
and for those who chose not to concentrate on the, quote-unquote,

18
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:41,160
badness that was going on both in the United States and abroad,

19
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:42,500
it was a fun time.

20
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:48,060
Yuppies, hackers, and valley girls created a new language and fashion,

21
00:01:48,061 --> 00:01:51,180
as well as the consumer culture that naturally followed.

22
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:56,780
The 80s really kind of came out of that whole concept from the 70s of let's go, let's go, let's go, let's go.

23
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:59,140
It was a decade of attention deficit disorder.

24
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:05,200
It was the amount of product that was out there, and people just kept buying and buying and buying.

25
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:08,820
At their core, all these items had one thing in common,

26
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:13,380
one very small thing that was getting smaller all the time,

27
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:17,460
the integrated circuit or microchip or simply the chip.

28
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:28,160
Anything that human beings interacted with could contain a microprocessor and could become, at least in a tiny way, intelligent.

29
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:37,900
And so in the 80s, that process first became possible and ubiquitous because the chips got so cheap.

30
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:41,220
Let's rewind, because we learned how in the 80s.

31
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:44,560
What exactly is a chip?

32
00:02:44,561 --> 00:02:49,560
A chip is a sliver of silicon, less than an inch square,

33
00:02:49,561 --> 00:02:54,560
onto which engineers etch microscopic transistors and connecting wires.

34
00:02:54,561 --> 00:02:59,560
The transistors act as switches that control the flow of currents in the wires.

35
00:02:59,561 --> 00:03:06,560
The silicon acts as a semiconductor, which allows the transistors to process the binary code of computers,

36
00:03:06,561 --> 00:03:09,560
on for one, off for zero.

37
00:03:09,561 --> 00:03:14,560
The number of transistors on the chip determines power and cost.

38
00:03:14,561 --> 00:03:16,560
This is a microchip.

39
00:03:16,561 --> 00:03:20,560
It is a Z80 processor, very common in the 1980s.

40
00:03:20,561 --> 00:03:24,560
It had maybe 10,000 transistors on it.

41
00:03:24,561 --> 00:03:29,560
And to understand how revolutionary that is, you can look at this board.

42
00:03:29,561 --> 00:03:32,560
This is what we used before there were microchips.

43
00:03:32,561 --> 00:03:35,560
Each one of these little cans is a transistor.

44
00:03:35,561 --> 00:03:43,560
What you've done is you've taken these big transistors and you've etched them on a tiny piece of silicon.

45
00:03:43,561 --> 00:03:51,560
And essentially you've had machines mass produce thousands and thousands of them for pennies each.

46
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:57,560
This is a wafer. It's a 300 millimeter 12 inch wafer.

47
00:03:57,561 --> 00:04:01,560
And if you look carefully you can see there are many squares on this wafer.

48
00:04:01,561 --> 00:04:04,560
Each one of these is a fully integrated circuit,

49
00:04:04,561 --> 00:04:08,560
and the cost of producing this wafer is constant.

50
00:04:08,561 --> 00:04:13,560
In 1965, you could only place 10 or 15, 20 components

51
00:04:13,561 --> 00:04:16,560
on a single piece of silicon.

52
00:04:16,561 --> 00:04:19,560
Today, the number of transistors that can fit on a single chip

53
00:04:19,561 --> 00:04:22,560
is in the billions.

54
00:04:22,561 --> 00:04:26,560
The rate of innovation was predicted by Moore's Law.

55
00:04:26,561 --> 00:04:28,560
Coined by one of the founders of Intel,

56
00:04:28,561 --> 00:04:37,780
Moore's Law basically states that integrated circuit complexity will double with respect to minimum component cost about every 18 months.

57
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,440
So as performance doubles, price remains the same.

58
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:47,200
The magic of Moore's Law was the fact that it happened on a regularly scheduled basis.

59
00:04:47,201 --> 00:04:53,540
So anybody who was trying to innovate, they would look and say, when will my product be able to come to the market?

60
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:56,180
Oh, it'll come in two and a half years.

61
00:04:56,181 --> 00:05:00,060
What will the capabilities of the silicon chip be in two and a half years?

62
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:04,060
And they would design their innovation to intersect that line

63
00:05:04,061 --> 00:05:07,800
rather than only innovating on what was available at the moment.

64
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:10,635
We're standing outside the Robert B.

65
00:05:10,636 --> 00:05:13,840
Noyce headquarters building of Intel Corporation.

66
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:18,900
Intel was founded in 1968 by Robert Noyce, Gordon Moore, and Andrew Grove.

67
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:22,540
They started out building memory products

68
00:05:22,541 --> 00:05:26,140
and along the way developed a product called the microprocessor.

69
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:31,720
The microprocessor eventually turned out to be the most important product for Intel.

70
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:36,580
Early computer products began hitting stores in the late 1970s.

71
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:43,420
In 1981, Intel's 8088 chip, containing 29,000 transistors,

72
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:49,100
was at the heart of an invention that would forever change the way humans interact with technology and business.

73
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:51,380
The IBM PC.

74
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:59,860
You had the TRS-80, the Commodore PET, and the Apple II.

75
00:05:59,861 --> 00:06:03,140
And they were very popular in middle schools, high schools.

76
00:06:03,141 --> 00:06:05,540
They didn't really get into business very much.

77
00:06:05,541 --> 00:06:09,460
People had a hard time taking these early computers seriously.

78
00:06:09,461 --> 00:06:14,980
It took the IBM PC for people to really look at the computer as something more than a toy.

79
00:06:14,981 --> 00:06:17,720
Now it's got those magical three letters, IBM.

80
00:06:17,721 --> 00:06:19,300
It must have something to do with business.

81
00:06:19,301 --> 00:06:21,380
IBM doesn't do frivolous things.

82
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:26,960
personal computers and the microprocessors needed to power them spawned a giant industry.

83
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:30,280
And this industry already had a home.

84
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:33,560
The area was dubbed Silicon Valley.

85
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:38,040
Silicon for the semiconductor element that made the chip work,

86
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:42,160
and Valley for its location in Northern California's Santa Clara Valley.

87
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:47,080
New tech corporations sprouted up next to established tech organizations,

88
00:06:47,081 --> 00:06:52,660
all growing exponentially in the 80s within an area of about 50 square miles.

89
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:23,120
Silicon Valley now boasts more millionaires per capita than any other region in the United States,

90
00:07:23,121 --> 00:07:27,220
and almost half of its adult residents hold college degrees.

91
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:32,140
Silicon Valley today is thought of as more than just a geographic area.

92
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:38,820
It's also a state of mind, typified by the lifestyle, the work ethic, the interchange of ideas.

93
00:07:38,821 --> 00:07:44,300
In the 80s, Silicon Valley was just starting into its real boom.

94
00:07:44,301 --> 00:07:54,840
Back then, it was this whole robust environment where everybody worked tech, talked tech, and everybody was out to change the world.

95
00:07:54,980 --> 00:07:59,300
It was like the gold rush times, but in the 80s instead of in 1849.

96
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:07,820
And everybody from around the world, whether they be from Pakistan or Peoria, they were all coming into Silicon Valley to try to make their mark.

97
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:13,780
And many of them did exactly that, especially a local kid named Steve Wozniak.

98
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:22,640
Steve Wozniak is one of my heroes because he really worked in a garage and made an object and then turned that object into a company.

99
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:26,840
And at the time, there was nothing like it.

100
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:29,520
It was a complete computer system.

101
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:32,600
Primitive, yes, but a complete system.

102
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:35,980
And then Steve Wozniak also wrote most of the software for us.

103
00:08:35,981 --> 00:08:38,000
So he was a hardware designer.

104
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:40,060
He was a problem solver.

105
00:08:40,061 --> 00:08:43,820
was a software developer, just kind of this incredible genius

106
00:08:43,821 --> 00:08:47,080
sort of guy who got the whole Apple Corporation started.

107
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:51,520
Waz, as he's known, and his partner Steve Jobs,

108
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:54,820
worked day and night to make their computing dreams a reality.

109
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:59,380
The suburban ranch house behind us is the site of the quintessential

110
00:08:59,381 --> 00:09:03,640
Valley Success Story. This is where Apple Computer was founded in 1976

111
00:09:03,641 --> 00:09:07,600
by Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak. They started off building

112
00:09:07,601 --> 00:09:09,960
Apple One computer boards in Jobs' bedroom,

113
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:11,480
and they moved out to the garage

114
00:09:11,481 --> 00:09:13,200
when they received their first production order.

115
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:18,880
That first order from a local retailer for 50 computers

116
00:09:18,881 --> 00:09:22,700
launched Apple and the Steves into tech geek glory.

117
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:27,160
I was thinking, how do I build the absolute finest,

118
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:28,940
best computer of the day?

119
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:32,640
And Steve was thinking, how can we somehow build a product

120
00:09:32,641 --> 00:09:35,060
that will reach the masses and reach people

121
00:09:35,061 --> 00:09:37,420
that want to pay money to get access to a computer

122
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:38,121
and to use it.

123
00:09:38,122 --> 00:09:41,600
And it was his idea to start a company and I was kind of like, well, I

124
00:09:41,601 --> 00:09:42,936
don't know how to start a company.

125
00:09:42,937 --> 00:09:44,940
I just want to, you know, design neat computers and

126
00:09:44,941 --> 00:09:45,775
things like that.

127
00:09:45,776 --> 00:09:49,260
So we kind of blended our insights, but we did have a goal that we were

128
00:09:49,261 --> 00:09:52,980
going to build a product that was so low cost it would improve a lot of people's lives,

129
00:09:52,981 --> 00:09:54,980
both at home and at work.

130
00:09:54,981 --> 00:10:00,167
The Apple I evolved into the Apple II, which most people remember.

131
00:10:00,168 --> 00:10:01,660
Then came the Lisa,

132
00:10:01,661 --> 00:10:03,453
which most people don't remember.

133
00:10:03,454 --> 00:10:06,660
It was Apple's first attempt at a graphical user interface.

134
00:10:06,661 --> 00:10:10,380
interface, or GUI.

135
00:10:10,381 --> 00:10:14,080
All the computers before had been command line driven.

136
00:10:14,081 --> 00:10:18,240
Let's say you wanted to copy a file from one floppy disk to another floppy disk.

137
00:10:18,241 --> 00:10:22,560
You would have to type in commands that specify the name of the floppy disk, which might be

138
00:10:22,561 --> 00:10:28,600
something so cryptic as C colon, D colon, or E colon, or F colon, and you'd have to type

139
00:10:28,601 --> 00:10:33,900
the name of a file, and you couldn't make one typing mistake.

140
00:10:33,901 --> 00:10:40,040
The GUI allowed the user to work with representative icons rather than memorized lines of code.

141
00:10:40,041 --> 00:10:43,280
When you're sitting at your desk thinking, how can I design a better computer, you're

142
00:10:43,281 --> 00:10:47,560
staring at your desk and there's folders and you've got your desktop and there's garbage

143
00:10:47,561 --> 00:10:48,560
can there.

144
00:10:48,561 --> 00:10:51,920
I can open and close windows and things talk to each other.

145
00:10:51,921 --> 00:10:56,060
I can move folders around and throw them in the garbage and rename them.

146
00:10:56,061 --> 00:11:01,260
Despite this user-friendly application, the Lisa never took off because most people couldn't

147
00:11:01,261 --> 00:11:03,860
afford to own one.

148
00:11:03,861 --> 00:11:07,000
It took a megabyte of memory to do that kind of computer,

149
00:11:07,001 --> 00:11:10,240
and a megabyte of memory back then just cost $5,000

150
00:11:10,241 --> 00:11:11,000
retail.

151
00:11:11,001 --> 00:11:13,280
So our computer sold for something like $10,000,

152
00:11:13,281 --> 00:11:15,300
and it wouldn't sell very well.

153
00:11:15,301 --> 00:11:18,280
But the price that's affordable has been almost the same

154
00:11:18,281 --> 00:11:22,880
since our first Apple II was kind of in this $2,000-ish price

155
00:11:22,881 --> 00:11:24,080
range.

156
00:11:24,081 --> 00:11:26,720
Steve Jobs pushed his engineers to come up with a GUI

157
00:11:26,721 --> 00:11:29,780
computer within this desired price range.

158
00:11:29,781 --> 00:11:30,840
And they succeeded.

159
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:34,860
Here you have, in 1984, the Apple Macintosh.

160
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:40,400
This came out to a lot of fanfare as a different kind of computer, quote, for the rest of us.

161
00:11:40,401 --> 00:11:46,180
So while the IBM PC was pitched at the sort of staid kind of boring businessman,

162
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:49,280
the Mac was for people who wanted to have fun, who were creative,

163
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:53,960
and who weren't computer experts but needed to do work with a computer quickly.

164
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:59,660
The Mac had a mouse and pulled down menus and picture icons.

165
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:01,640
that users could click and drag.

166
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,300
Soon, IBM models also had these features.

167
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:10,000
Learning to use computers went from daunting to doable.

168
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:14,840
In the 1980s, if you were using a personal computer,

169
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:16,700
you were a beginner by definition,

170
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:18,820
and people really needed guidance.

171
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:21,520
And the fact that there were magazines like this

172
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:23,740
and books and other sources of information

173
00:12:23,741 --> 00:12:26,120
was really a sign that the revolution was here to stay.

174
00:12:26,121 --> 00:12:30,120
Apple computers made Woz a multimillionaire.

175
00:12:30,121 --> 00:12:35,120
He took his money and created one of the biggest musical events of the 1980s,

176
00:12:35,121 --> 00:12:37,120
the US Festival.

177
00:12:37,121 --> 00:12:45,120
We put $10 million into building an amphitheater and putting in various needed facilities and plumbing and whatever.

178
00:12:45,121 --> 00:12:47,120
I even tested the porta potties myself.

179
00:12:47,121 --> 00:12:53,120
I think the reason it's gone over so well is that we got more porta potties per person than any other event of its type ever.

180
00:12:53,121 --> 00:12:56,120
And we put on the concert and it would run for three days.

181
00:12:56,121 --> 00:12:58,120
We had technology tents.

182
00:12:58,121 --> 00:12:59,120
This was in the early 80s.

183
00:12:59,121 --> 00:13:01,120
And Apple had kicked off this big thing,

184
00:13:01,121 --> 00:13:04,120
and now there were other companies building personal computers,

185
00:13:04,121 --> 00:13:06,120
and it was becoming a part of our life.

186
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:11,120
And personal computers were just starting to connect

187
00:13:11,121 --> 00:13:15,120
to musical instruments, and technology and music kind of go together,

188
00:13:15,121 --> 00:13:16,120
and they always do.

189
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:22,120
So I had a technology fair, and companies basically showed off their products

190
00:13:22,121 --> 00:13:25,320
products and what they could do, and you walked through and got

191
00:13:25,321 --> 00:13:27,800
educated. The Aus Festival

192
00:13:27,801 --> 00:13:31,160
foreshadowed the marriage of culture and technology, made

193
00:13:31,161 --> 00:13:36,760
possible by the microprocessor. The chip transformed the way

194
00:13:36,761 --> 00:13:41,560
people did business, and it also transformed the way people did

195
00:13:41,561 --> 00:13:45,240
pleasure. After punching out for the day,

196
00:13:45,241 --> 00:13:49,320
we found all kinds of techy new ways to spend our downtime in the

197
00:13:49,321 --> 00:13:54,020
in the 80s.

198
00:13:54,021 --> 00:14:00,820
When Apple went public in 1980, it generated more money than any other IPO since 1956,

199
00:14:00,821 --> 00:14:07,920
nearly $1.8 billion, and created more instant millionaires than any company in history.

200
00:14:07,921 --> 00:14:14,160
80s tech will return on Modern Marvels.

201
00:14:14,161 --> 00:14:18,980
If you were lucky enough to be a kid in the 80s, you might remember spending your allowance

202
00:14:18,981 --> 00:14:22,880
at the virtual nirvana called the arcade,

203
00:14:22,881 --> 00:14:26,620
bound only by the limits of your imagination

204
00:14:26,621 --> 00:14:27,860
and how many quarters you had.

205
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:36,380
Once just a place to play games like skee-ball and pinball,

206
00:14:36,381 --> 00:14:40,500
arcades now boasted the newly popular video combat games,

207
00:14:40,501 --> 00:14:43,680
where you could single-handedly save the world from aliens

208
00:14:43,681 --> 00:14:46,020
by repeatedly pressing a button.

209
00:14:46,021 --> 00:14:48,940
But even more popular were character-driven games,

210
00:14:48,941 --> 00:14:50,700
like Donkey Kong and Pac-Man.

211
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:57,700
According to Pac-Man's creator, Toru Iwatani,

212
00:14:57,701 --> 00:15:00,180
the inspiration for the character came from a pizza

213
00:15:00,181 --> 00:15:01,280
with a slice missing.

214
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:09,220
If you long for the games of days gone by,

215
00:15:09,221 --> 00:15:12,760
the vintage arcade superstore in Glendale, California

216
00:15:12,761 --> 00:15:14,480
can resurrect your childhood

217
00:15:14,481 --> 00:15:17,100
with one of their restored arcade games.

218
00:15:17,101 --> 00:15:18,660
Came up 500 short.

219
00:15:18,661 --> 00:15:21,460
Most of our customers are people that played these games when they were kids.

220
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:22,940
Some of our customers are collectors.

221
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:25,580
We have some customers that are celebrities.

222
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:28,820
Our most sought after products are the early 80s video games,

223
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:32,320
like Ms. Pac-Man, Galaga, Defender, Asteroids, Donkey Kong.

224
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:35,300
If we don't have something, we can find it for you.

225
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:37,380
We completely go through them.

226
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:38,680
We redo the control panels.

227
00:15:38,820 --> 00:15:43,000
We put new monitor class on them, new marquees, new T-molding, repaint the sides.

228
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:45,300
A lot of times we put new side art on them.

229
00:15:45,420 --> 00:15:46,660
We can put new monitors in them.

230
00:15:46,661 --> 00:15:51,100
Of course, Gene personally tests each game before it's shipped out to the customer.

231
00:15:54,680 --> 00:16:00,680
Inside, arcade games are fairly simple machines, made possible by our old friend, the microprocessor.

232
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,480
Power comes through the cord, goes in this transformer, there's some fuses.

233
00:16:05,800 --> 00:16:06,720
This is the power supply.

234
00:16:07,380 --> 00:16:08,460
Here's the computer board.

235
00:16:09,200 --> 00:16:10,580
Here's the memory chips right here.

