Here's the monitor that displays the image.

238
00:16:15,461 --> 00:16:21,080
And the speakers up here, the control panel's in the front, and that's basically what there is in a video game.

239
00:16:21,960 --> 00:16:26,080
Arcade game technology helped mesmerize an impressionable new generation.

240
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:33,660
And like many electronics in the 80s, the games became small and cheap enough to come home.

241
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:40,980
Sales of home video consoles like the Atari 2600 boomed.

242
00:16:40,981 --> 00:16:47,060
There was some interest on the part of many parents in acquiring home games so that their

243
00:16:47,061 --> 00:16:51,060
children would be playing at home as opposed to going out into this unknown space of the

244
00:16:51,061 --> 00:16:54,600
arcade where the parents would lose control over their children.

245
00:16:54,601 --> 00:16:59,820
From the kids' point of view, the games that were conversions of popular arcade games for

246
00:16:59,821 --> 00:17:06,500
home consoles tended to be inferior, technically speaking.

247
00:17:06,501 --> 00:17:10,380
Whether or not, home video games were a hit.

248
00:17:10,480 --> 00:17:13,580
Atari founder Nolan Bushnell seemed to have discovered

249
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:18,040
a formula that worked, at least for a while.

250
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:22,080
A 2600 contains a very, very simple microprocessor

251
00:17:22,180 --> 00:17:24,460
running at maybe 1 megahertz.

252
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:26,860
And that primitive nature allowed it

253
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:29,760
to handle only games that were two-dimensional

254
00:17:29,860 --> 00:17:32,300
with just a few colors and a few little objects

255
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:34,260
that were moving on the screen.

256
00:17:34,360 --> 00:17:36,500
Bushnell allowed an open culture of game development.

257
00:17:36,501 --> 00:17:40,860
meaning that other companies could produce cartridges that were compatible with the 2600.

258
00:17:42,180 --> 00:17:44,920
Some of the games weren't exactly top-notch.

259
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:50,900
As a result of that, Atari, in a way, kind of lost control of that platform.

260
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:54,700
That led in the early 1980s to kind of a collapse of the industry.

261
00:17:56,420 --> 00:18:01,080
The game makers that followed, like Nintendo, learned from Atari's mistakes.

262
00:18:01,081 --> 00:18:07,080
They patented and coded their consoles and insisted on strict quality control guidelines.

263
00:18:07,081 --> 00:18:11,080
Despite some awesome innovations in video games and graphics over the years,

264
00:18:11,081 --> 00:18:16,080
we still look back nostalgically on those games from the 80s.

265
00:18:18,080 --> 00:18:21,080
If you poll, say, university students today,

266
00:18:21,081 --> 00:18:26,080
more than 50% of them have played with an Atari 2600 still to this day.

267
00:18:26,081 --> 00:18:30,080
For some reason, it's maintained its popularity.

268
00:18:30,081 --> 00:18:34,320
The Atari 2600 nailed a certain kind of interactive play

269
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:38,320
experience that's possible on a very simple platform.

270
00:18:38,420 --> 00:18:40,820
They're really timeless games.

271
00:18:40,920 --> 00:18:42,460
If games weren't your thing, then you

272
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:45,820
may remember other television-related breakthroughs.

273
00:18:45,920 --> 00:18:51,240
MTV, CNN, and HBO were a few of the specialty cable channels

274
00:18:51,340 --> 00:18:53,540
making a big splash on the tube.

275
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:56,580
But you couldn't possibly watch everything.

276
00:18:56,680 --> 00:18:58,240
Or could you?

277
00:18:58,340 --> 00:19:00,080
Enter the VCR.

278
00:19:00,081 --> 00:19:06,920
It allowed people to capture television and not have to schedule their lives around their favorite program anymore,

279
00:19:06,921 --> 00:19:12,020
because now they could go and throw a tape in the machine, set the timer, record, and go out and do whatever they needed to do.

280
00:19:12,460 --> 00:19:15,460
Despite the fact that the clock always seemed to think it was midnight,

281
00:19:16,300 --> 00:19:21,240
VCRs enabled us to record a show at its regularly scheduled time and then watch it later.

282
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:26,200
And it sounds so simple today, but that was a big deal at that time.

283
00:19:26,380 --> 00:19:28,980
The phenomena that resulted was this notion of time shifting.

284
00:19:28,981 --> 00:19:32,540
Of course, the other thing that happened, people found the fast-forward button,

285
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:36,620
and then they could skip through commercials or parts of a program they weren't interested in.

286
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:40,760
Consumer VCRs were introduced in the mid-1970s,

287
00:19:40,860 --> 00:19:44,200
but few households actually had one until the 1980s.

288
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:50,840
In 1990, over 200 million VCRs were sold worldwide.

289
00:19:51,700 --> 00:19:54,420
When you were buying one in the early days, you had a big decision,

290
00:19:54,421 --> 00:19:59,560
which was, did you want beta, which was Sony's tape format,

291
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:02,920
or VHS, which was from JVC,

292
00:20:03,280 --> 00:20:06,040
and the two formats were completely incompatible.

293
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:11,040
The differences between the two technologies led to a format war,

294
00:20:11,260 --> 00:20:13,460
and only one would be left standing.

295
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:17,300
Both machines essentially did the same things,

296
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,740
record and playback video after a cassette was inserted.

297
00:20:20,741 --> 00:20:28,340
In record mode, a VCR uses a rotating recording head that makes contact with the tape inside a cassette.

298
00:20:29,860 --> 00:20:36,960
As the tape moves past the spinning head, it records an analog signal of each frame as a diagonal stripe on the tape.

299
00:20:38,100 --> 00:20:41,680
Another head records sound and tracking information on the edge of the tape.

300
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:49,520
In playback mode, the rotating head lines itself up with the diagonal stripes by using the tape's tracking codes.

301
00:20:50,740 --> 00:20:56,500
As the head rotates over the stripe, it reads the information for the image and sends it to the TV.

302
00:20:57,660 --> 00:20:59,580
A separate head reads the sound.

303
00:21:00,820 --> 00:21:04,380
The similarity between Betamax and VHS ends there.

304
00:21:05,180 --> 00:21:11,540
Betamax had slightly better quality because it used a larger head to read the tape.

305
00:21:11,541 --> 00:21:17,100
And that larger head meant that they could put a little more detail on the tape and get a little better clarity.

306
00:21:17,101 --> 00:21:21,260
and that limited the tape's maximum length to 60 minutes

307
00:21:21,261 --> 00:21:23,660
when the Betamax decks first came out.

308
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:27,780
VHS was able to fit up to two hours on a tape,

309
00:21:28,300 --> 00:21:32,080
and because most movies fit in a two-hour tape,

310
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:36,220
that gave JVC a big advantage in the home rental market.

311
00:21:36,740 --> 00:21:39,620
Video rental stores popped up on every street corner,

312
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:42,820
and it soon became common to rent a movie on tape

313
00:21:42,821 --> 00:21:44,260
and watch it from your couch.

314
00:21:44,261 --> 00:21:47,240
unlike Sony

315
00:21:47,241 --> 00:21:50,820
JVC saw an advantage to licensing their technology

316
00:21:50,821 --> 00:21:52,820
to many other electronics manufacturers

317
00:21:52,821 --> 00:21:56,820
the competition lowered VHS prices

318
00:21:56,821 --> 00:21:58,620
attracting more and more buyers

319
00:21:58,621 --> 00:22:02,520
and there were two other reasons

320
00:22:02,521 --> 00:22:04,380
that VHS emerged victorious

321
00:22:04,381 --> 00:22:06,920
VHS tapes were cheaper

322
00:22:06,921 --> 00:22:10,060
and they were more widely accepted in the marketplace

323
00:22:10,061 --> 00:22:13,720
and then there was a force that isn't talked about so much

324
00:22:13,721 --> 00:22:19,240
And that's the fact that most video stores had the front part of the store where you rented Hollywood movies

325
00:22:19,241 --> 00:22:24,740
and then a back room where you could rent other material of a pornographic nature.

326
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:29,440
This was the first time that people could actually watch these works of art in their own homes.

327
00:22:29,441 --> 00:22:33,160
And that caused an explosion in that particular industry.

328
00:22:33,740 --> 00:22:36,740
Since that industry pretty much standardized on VHS,

329
00:22:37,020 --> 00:22:41,060
it drove a lot of what was happening in the front part of the video store as well.

330
00:22:41,061 --> 00:22:45,860
It was the final nail in the coffin for the Betamax home VCR market.

331
00:22:48,300 --> 00:22:50,620
80s technology helped us kick back at home.

332
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:56,200
But products like the Walkman and Cell Phone showed us that we could get tech to go.

333
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:04,360
By the end of 1981, about 100,000 Pac-Man arcade machines had been sold.

334
00:23:05,660 --> 00:23:08,140
80s tech will return on Modern Marvels.

335
00:23:11,060 --> 00:23:17,580
All the multifunctional items we hold so dear today have their roots in simpler products

336
00:23:17,581 --> 00:23:23,000
that became ubiquitous in the 80s, many of which were created by Japanese electronics

337
00:23:23,001 --> 00:23:23,701
firms.

338
00:23:24,260 --> 00:23:30,060
In the 80s, you saw the advancement of a whole bunch of devices from Sony, from the Walkman

339
00:23:30,061 --> 00:23:34,060
to the CD player, the VCR, the Handycam camcorder.

340
00:23:34,061 --> 00:23:41,740
and these devices were paving new ground in terms of innovation, but also design.

341
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:46,420
And then we saw other Japanese-based electronics companies follow Sony.

342
00:23:46,540 --> 00:23:51,540
In fact, there are one or two that have sort of referred to Sony as their R&D department.

343
00:23:52,220 --> 00:23:57,380
Nowhere was Sony's leadership more pronounced than in the realm of music reproduction technology.

344
00:23:57,381 --> 00:23:59,660
You started with home stereo systems.

345
00:23:59,661 --> 00:24:05,440
Those were shrunk down to a boom box, which you would carry on your shoulder and blast everyone around you.

346
00:24:05,860 --> 00:24:10,740
And then came this, the Sony Walkman, which was just a total revolution.

347
00:24:11,240 --> 00:24:17,620
You could take your own songs, mix them onto a cassette, load them into a Walkman, stick it in your pocket.

348
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:20,800
The headphones made it sound incredibly good.

349
00:24:21,160 --> 00:24:27,720
It was made possible by magnet technology that allowed them to shrink the headphones down to a very small, lightweight package.

350
00:24:27,721 --> 00:24:34,100
Standard headphones of the time weighed about 14 ounces and covered the entire ear.

351
00:24:35,100 --> 00:24:45,240
Sony was able to miniaturize headphones to about 1.5 ounces by replacing ferrite magnets with much stronger and smaller samarium cobalt magnets.

352
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:51,500
The Walkman sends an electrical signal carrying the sound wave that the headset must reproduce.

353
00:24:51,501 --> 00:24:55,100
The signal creates a magnetic field in the coil that attracts

354
00:24:55,101 --> 00:24:57,500
or repels the samarium cobalt magnet.

355
00:24:57,501 --> 00:25:01,500
The coil's movement causes the headset's diaphragm to vibrate,

356
00:25:01,501 --> 00:25:04,500
creating the sound waves that you hear.

357
00:25:04,501 --> 00:25:08,500
It was the first time people could listen to music,

358
00:25:08,501 --> 00:25:12,500
any music they wanted to, anywhere, anytime, anyplace,

359
00:25:12,501 --> 00:25:15,500
frankly, without disturbing anybody else.

360
00:25:15,501 --> 00:25:19,500
It seems very simple today, but at the time it was pretty revolutionary,

361
00:25:19,501 --> 00:25:22,500
revolutionary, and it really did create a whole movement in

362
00:25:22,540 --> 00:25:27,500
terms of lifestyles and behaviors and the way people

363
00:25:27,540 --> 00:25:29,500
entertain themselves.

364
00:25:29,540 --> 00:25:32,420
But you still had to carry around those clunky tapes

365
00:25:32,440 --> 00:25:35,420
that degraded over time.

366
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:39,460
Even the Walkman couldn't save the ill-fated cassette,

367
00:25:39,480 --> 00:25:42,500
because in 1982, Sony, with some help from Philips

368
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:47,500
Electronics, upped the ante yet again with the compact disc.

369
00:25:47,501 --> 00:25:53,000
For decades we had wax and then vinyl discs and then we saw the 8-track tape

370
00:25:53,001 --> 00:25:57,000
which was eventually obliterated by the cassette tape and as we come into the

371
00:25:57,001 --> 00:26:02,140
beginning of the 1980s we're left with two common formats the cassette tape and

372
00:26:02,141 --> 00:26:09,660
the vinyl LP. These were both replaced in the early 1980s by this the compact

373
00:26:09,661 --> 00:26:15,940
disc. The CD is a total revolution and it's a revolution for two reasons. First

374
00:26:15,941 --> 00:26:20,700
First of all, the information stored on the CD is stored digitally,

375
00:26:20,701 --> 00:26:25,280
so that each time you play the CD, you're going to hear exactly the same sound.

376
00:26:25,281 --> 00:26:31,940
And the second reason it's revolutionary is because so much information is stored on the CD compared to a vinyl record.

377
00:26:31,941 --> 00:26:38,580
It was Sony who specified that the CD would be able to hold at least 74 minutes of music

378
00:26:38,581 --> 00:26:43,900
because one of our top executives at the time, Noria Oga, was also a conductor

379
00:26:43,901 --> 00:26:46,140
and also very interested in classical music

380
00:26:46,141 --> 00:26:48,460
and wanted Beethoven's Ninth Symphony

381
00:26:48,461 --> 00:26:50,520
to be able to fit on a CD.

382
00:26:52,780 --> 00:26:56,800
A CD starts as a disc 1.2 millimeters thick,

383
00:26:56,801 --> 00:26:58,920
made of clear polycarbonate plastic.

384
00:27:00,400 --> 00:27:04,380
During manufacturing, billions of pits or scratches

385
00:27:04,381 --> 00:27:07,320
are pressed into the plastic in a spiral string.

386
00:27:07,321 --> 00:27:09,240
The disc is then coated on one side

387
00:27:09,241 --> 00:27:11,700
with so-called super purity aluminum

388
00:27:11,701 --> 00:27:13,780
to create a mirrored surface.

389
00:27:13,781 --> 00:27:17,880
A film of lacquer protects the aluminum and is painted with a label for the CD.

390
00:27:19,280 --> 00:27:25,660
A CD is read by focusing a semiconductor laser through the transparent bottom of the polycarbonate layer.

391
00:27:26,360 --> 00:27:32,340
If you were able to grab a hold of that little string somehow and peel it off,

392
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:38,080
you'd find that it's a string half a micron thick, but three miles long.

393
00:27:38,081 --> 00:27:44,560
The laser beam hits the CD, and it's going to find one of two things when it hits that surface.

394
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:48,680
It's either going to find actual mirror, and it's going to reflect back clearly,

395
00:27:48,920 --> 00:27:54,720
or it's going to find one of those tiny scratches, and it's going to get diffused when it reflects off.

396
00:27:55,240 --> 00:28:01,340
Now, the CD reader is able to tell the difference between actual mirror and scratch,

397
00:28:01,341 --> 00:28:08,640
and from that decide what bits of information have been written onto that CD surface.

398
00:28:09,280 --> 00:28:11,360
Of course, you needed a CD player.

399
00:28:12,660 --> 00:28:16,060
Sony's CDP-101 was also introduced in 1982.

400
00:28:17,300 --> 00:28:22,160
Now the trick was to convince consumers to repurchase all their music on the new format.

401
00:28:23,080 --> 00:28:28,380
But as people heard it or visited with friends or somehow came in touch with CDs

402
00:28:28,381 --> 00:28:33,940
and the quality of the music and the amazing fact that it would always have this great quality.

403
00:28:34,660 --> 00:28:36,680
It got adopted very quickly.

404
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:42,720
Also, we came out later with the Discman, which was a CD-based version of the Walkman,

405
00:28:43,080 --> 00:28:47,140
making CD a medium for portable music enjoyment.

406
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:51,160
While many used technology to retreat into their own worlds,

407
00:28:51,780 --> 00:28:53,840
some embraced it for more social reasons.

408
00:28:53,841 --> 00:28:57,840
The first commercial portable cellular telephone,

409
00:28:57,841 --> 00:29:03,840
Motorola's Dynatac 8000X, showed us that we could stay in touch on the go.

410
00:29:03,841 --> 00:29:08,840
You had the normal telephone, then you had the car phone,

411
00:29:08,841 --> 00:29:11,840
and then you had the beginning of the portable cell phone.

412
00:29:11,841 --> 00:29:16,840
And we look at this now as totally comical because it's so gigantic.

413
00:29:16,841 --> 00:29:19,840
It weighs about two pounds, but this, at the time,

414
00:29:19,841 --> 00:29:22,840
was possibly the coolest object that a person could own.

415
00:29:22,841 --> 00:29:27,740
It cost about $4,000, maybe a dollar or two per minute to use it.

416
00:29:28,140 --> 00:29:31,560
But it had no cord, and that's what mattered most.

417
00:29:32,100 --> 00:29:36,460
Like modern cell phones, the brick was basically a sophisticated radio system.

418
00:29:37,580 --> 00:29:42,480
When you dial a call, your phone sends a radio message to a cell phone tower nearby.

419
00:29:43,520 --> 00:29:46,080
The tower sets up the call with two frequencies,

420
00:29:46,700 --> 00:29:50,960
one frequency for your voice and the other for the voice of the person you're talking to.

421
00:29:51,760 --> 00:29:55,360
This radio system can transmit on hundreds of different radio frequencies,

422
00:29:55,880 --> 00:30:00,800
so each tower can handle dozens of calls at once without one call interfering with another.

423
00:30:01,480 --> 00:30:07,240
If a phone is moving, its signal transfers from one tower to the next, spaced about two miles apart.

424
00:30:08,160 --> 00:30:11,300
Cell phones eventually went from analog signals to digital,

425
00:30:11,840 --> 00:30:15,960
and like everything else, they got smaller, faster, and thankfully cheaper.

426
00:30:17,260 --> 00:30:20,680
Prompting that all-important 80s question, who are you going to call?

427
00:30:20,960 --> 00:30:24,960
Pocket Tech was here to stay, and it proliferated into all

428
00:30:24,961 --> 00:30:27,960
forms of entertainment and communication.

429
00:30:27,961 --> 00:30:29,960
But was there a downside?

430
00:30:29,961 --> 00:30:32,960
The more technology we get, the more it becomes embedded in

431
00:30:32,961 --> 00:30:35,960
us, the more we're plugged into our iPods, the more we're on

432
00:30:35,961 --> 00:30:37,960
the cell phone.

433
00:30:37,961 --> 00:30:40,960
Maybe there's less and less reality in our lives and more

434
00:30:40,961 --> 00:30:44,960
and more mediation of reality through these electronic devices.

435
00:30:44,961 --> 00:30:48,960
The 80s got us digitized, downsized, and on the go.

436
00:30:48,961 --> 00:30:52,460
When we had so many toys, we needed a cool car to transport

437
00:30:52,461 --> 00:30:53,460
them all.

438
00:30:53,461 --> 00:30:57,460
And in 1981, a visionary vehicle showed us a portal

439
00:30:57,461 --> 00:30:58,460
to the future.

440
00:31:00,960 --> 00:31:03,460
Sony's first personal cassette player was known initially

441
00:31:03,461 --> 00:31:05,960
as the Walkman in Japan only.

442
00:31:05,961 --> 00:31:08,960
It was originally called the Stowaway in England,

443
00:31:08,961 --> 00:31:13,960
the Freestyle in Australia, and the Soundabout in the US.

444
00:31:13,961 --> 00:31:18,460
80s Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

445
00:31:18,960 --> 00:31:32,960
The DeLorean DMC-12 is still one of the coolest high-tech looking cars on the road,

446
00:31:34,620 --> 00:31:37,060
and it hasn't been manufactured since 1983.

447
00:31:37,660 --> 00:31:43,440
This is an exceptional car. It's a very sleek, sexy looking automobile.

448
00:31:43,441 --> 00:31:48,440
It performs well.

449
00:31:48,441 --> 00:31:55,720
When you drive it, you get looks from people that you don't get from any other car.

450
00:31:55,721 --> 00:31:59,940
I don't know how many times I've had people with cell phones take pictures of this car.

451
00:31:59,941 --> 00:32:04,660
The DeLorean was unlike any other car on the market in the 80s.

452
00:32:04,661 --> 00:32:10,000
The oil crisis of the 70s prompted many automakers to start building smaller, fuel efficient econo

453
00:32:10,001 --> 00:32:13,832
boxes that some believe left style at the curb.

454
00:32:13,833 --> 00:32:17,500
But the DeLorean was first class all the way.

455
00:32:18,700 --> 00:32:24,520
The brainchild of noted auto designer John DeLorean, the DMC-12 was developed for the U.S.

456
00:32:24,521 --> 00:32:28,660
market, but for financial reasons it was produced in Northern Ireland.

457
00:32:29,680 --> 00:32:34,800
The project was funded by a series of small investors, people like Johnny Carson, Sammy

458
00:32:34,801 --> 00:32:36,160
Davis Jr., some of John's friends.

459
00:32:36,161 --> 00:32:38,280
The big money came from the actual British government

460
00:32:38,281 --> 00:32:40,260
to the tune of about $150 million.

461
00:32:42,360 --> 00:32:45,200
By building the car in Belfast, Northern Ireland,

462
00:32:45,600 --> 00:32:49,860
they had hoped to give people jobs where unemployment was at the rate of about 30%, 40%.

463
00:32:49,861 --> 00:32:53,880
And with all the sectarian problems in Northern Ireland about that time,

464
00:32:54,100 --> 00:32:57,120
people had jobs and they're less likely to be causing trouble in the streets.

465
00:32:57,720 --> 00:33:01,580
When the car hit showrooms in 1981, shoppers were amazed.

466
00:33:02,760 --> 00:33:04,400
Initial sales were fantastic.

